<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aelius Gallus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 26–24 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aelius <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-gall">Gallus</a> was the second Roman prefect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, appointed by Augustus. In 26–25 BCE he led an expedition into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia-felix">Arabia Felix</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, aimed at the rich kingdoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> trade. Disease, heat and the poor guidance of the Nabataean minister Syllaeus ruined his army, which withdrew after failing to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marib">Marib</a>. It was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s only major attempt to conquer southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gaius Aelius Gallus succeeded Cornelius Gallus as the prefect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under Augustus, holding the office from 26 to 24 BCE; the two men have often been confused. He was a close friend of the Greek geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, who drew most of his information about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> from him, and he has been identified with the Aelius Gallus often quoted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galen">Galen</a>, whose remedies are said to have been used with success during the Arabian campaign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Augustus ordered the expedition partly to explore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and its peoples and partly to make treaties of friendship with them or to subdue them if they resisted, for the country was believed to be full of treasures. Accounts of it are given by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>. Gallus set out with 10,000 infantry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and allies, among them five hundred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and one thousand Nabataeans, and trusted the guidance of the Nabataean Syllaeus, who in Strabo's account deceived and misled him. After crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> the army landed at Leuke Kome after fourteen days and went on by foot and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> through the land of Aretas and a waste called Ararene to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najran">Najran</a>.</p><p>Six days further on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> fought the local people at a river and, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, killed 10,000 of them. They took Asca, which its king had abandoned, and Athrula without a struggle, garrisoned it and laid in grain and dates, then advanced on a city called Marsiaba, which the historian Guglielmo Ferrero identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marib">Marib</a>. Burning heat, bad water and want of supplies brought on a disease unknown to the Romans that destroyed most of the army. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> then drove the Romans even from the ground they had won. After six months in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, Gallus retreated in sixty days to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, having lost the greater part of his force.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Augustus recalled Gallus for failing to pacify the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushites</a> and replaced him with Gaius Petronius, a military commander and close friend of the emperor. The campaign later became the subject of a 1951 novel, The Eagle and the Sun, by Lord Belhaven, who had served in the region.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aelius Gallus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 26–25 BCE a Roman expedition under <b>Aelius Gallus</b>, prefect of Egypt, marched into south Arabia, besieged Marib and withdrew after heavy losses from disease and hardship; the geographer Strabo, a friend of Gallus, described the campaign.</blockquote><a href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 25 BCE a Roman expedition under <b>Aelius Gallus</b>, prefect of Egypt, marched into Arabia Felix with Nabataean guides and besieged Marib, which Strabo calls Mariaba, for six days before withdrawing for lack of water.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Aelius Gallus</b> led a failed expedition into Arabia in 26–25 BCE, and when the Kushites raided the south and carried off statues of Augustus, the prefect Gaius Petronius marched up the Nile and sacked Napata in about 24 BCE, before a settlement at Samos in 21–20 BCE fixed…</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Military</a></li></ul></section>
